From 34281b1efc0bee12dff01c4eb916eb7440754e93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 17:33:09 +0800
Subject: [caplifive-buildroot PATCH 4/4] Let a domain declare what it needs,
 and give it two regions instead of one

package/modcapstone/{module/capstone.c,include/capstone.h,userspace/lib/libcapstone.c}
components/opensbi (submodule), components/linux (submodule), configs/kernel.config, Makefile

Two changes that belong together, because either alone leaves the ceiling in place.

DECLARED REQUIREMENT. The module sized a domain by max(2 * code_len, 512 KiB) of
headroom. That is a proxy: the stack is whatever survives the gp-captable carve, the
carve grows with the image, so the rule compensates through code size. It is not
causally tied to recursion depth and it is loose in both directions -- a 10 KB control
domain got 512 KB of headroom, and a 2.9 MB interpreter got 12.4 MB of stack it never
asked for, measured on its own image. That 3x rule is what pushed an interpreter past
the buddy allocator's maximum order in the first place.

So the image states its requirement in an optional .capstone_domreq section, the
loader reads it out of the file, and the module uses it. Absent section keeps the old
rule byte for byte, so all 570 existing domains are untouched. The fields in
ioctl_dom_create_args are the "TODO: pass info about the required data region size"
that had been sitting in capstone.h. They are cleared explicitly in the loader because
struct ElfCode is an uninitialised stack variable in every caller -- otherwise an
undeclared image would hand the module stack garbage and it would size a region from
it.

TWO REGIONS. A declaring image gets its code and its data in separate allocations. The
monitor change that makes this possible is in components/opensbi. The coupling it
removes is the whole problem: one capability SPLIT into code | seal | data forces one
contiguous block, so the allocator's maximum order capped code_len at 1,376,256 bytes.
Against 79 run logs the largest domain ever created here is 1,375,688 -- 568 bytes
under it -- and neither the JerryScript nor the mruby port has ever been created.

Both regions stay power-of-two on purpose. Capability bounds are compressed (base and
length quantised to 2^(E+3), base rounding down and end up), so a 2^k-sized, 2^k-aligned
region is exactly representable while an arbitrary byte length is not; QEMU hides that
by shadowing exact bounds, silicon cannot. Exact sizing needs the granule arithmetic
with it and is not what this change is for.

CONFIG_ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER=13 is REVERTED, along with the arch/riscv Kconfig symbol it
needed, because this replaces it. Raising the ceiling meant carrying a Linux source
patch that upstream would not take -- riscv deliberately has no such symbol, and neither
do x86 or s390. Two regions of 4 MiB each are inside the stock allocator's reach.

MAKEFILE. The generated monitor assembly is gitignored, and the rule that produces it
named only the one-line wrapper that #includes the real source. Editing the monitor
therefore produced a firmware containing the old monitor, silently and consistently.
Naming the included source as a prerequisite fixes it; controlled both ways, make now
regenerates after a source edit and does not after an artifact touch.

Verified in QEMU on a matched pair differing only in whether the image declares:
the control took one 1,048,576-byte region, the declared build took a 131,072-byte
code region and a 524,288-byte data region at unrelated physical addresses, the
globals template landed in the data region, and both returned the same checksum.
---
 Makefile                                      | 12 ++-
 components/linux                              |  2 +-
 components/opensbi                            |  2 +-
 configs/kernel.config                         |  4 -
 package/modcapstone/include/capstone.h        |  8 +-
 package/modcapstone/module/capstone.c         | 83 ++++++++++++++++---
 .../modcapstone/userspace/lib/libcapstone.c   | 42 +++++++++-
 7 files changed, 131 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 8ec73b9..2db6731 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -23,8 +23,18 @@ build: $(CAPSTONE_S_OUTPUT)
 	fi
 
 
+# The .c files here are one-line wrappers that #include the real monitor source,
+# CAPSTONE_S_INPUT. Without naming it as a prerequisite, editing the monitor left the
+# generated assembly untouched -- and it is gitignored, so it is a build artifact that
+# the build would not rebuild. The firmware then compiles the OLD monitor and every
+# check downstream passes, because the image is internally consistent and simply does
+# not contain the change. Cost one wrong bisect on 2026-08-19.
+$(CAPSTONE_S_OUTPUT): $(CAPSTONE_S_INPUT)
+
+# $< not $^: the extra prerequisite above would otherwise be passed to the compiler as
+# a second input file.
 $(CAPSTONE_S_OUTPUT):%.c.S:%.c
-	cd "$(CAPSTONE_CC_PATH)" && if ! /bin/sh -c 'cargo run -- --abi capstone $^ -- -I"$(CAPSTONE_S_INCLUDE)" -D__riscv_xlen=64 > "$@"'; then \
+	cd "$(CAPSTONE_CC_PATH)" && if ! /bin/sh -c 'cargo run -- --abi capstone $< -- -I"$(CAPSTONE_S_INCLUDE)" -D__riscv_xlen=64 > "$@"'; then \
 		rm -f "$@"; \
 		echo "Compilation error. Make sure you supply the correct Capstone-C compiler directory path in CAPSTONE_CC_PATH" >&2; \
 		false; \
